<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    Provenance + license metadata. The extension ships as a folder, not a NuGet package, so these are
    informational rather than packaging-critical, but they keep authorship and the AGPL-3.0 license
    honest in the assembly + any future packaging. RepositoryUrl mirrors the manifest/class Url.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Authors>alextomas955</Authors>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/alextomas955/cove-extensions</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>AGPL-3.0-or-later</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="extension.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" CopyToPublishDirectory="Always" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Shared first-party code (generic options store, minimal-API permission gate, JSON factory). Copies
    local and ships bundled as Cove.Extensions.Shared.dll — it is NOT Private=false (that is only for the
    host-provided Cove refs) and is absent from the host-assembly strip denylist, so it survives the strip.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\..\..\shared\Cove.Extensions.Shared\Cove.Extensions.Shared.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Compile-time enforcement of the repo-wide bans in the root BannedSymbols.txt (wired once as an
    AdditionalFiles item in Directory.Build.props). PrivateAssets="all" keeps the analyzer out of the
    publish set: it has no runtime role and strip-verify asserts what ships.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The filesystem-mutation bans are project-local, and that scope is the point. This assembly has no
    legitimate filesystem mutation at all — its only I/O against a library root is the root guard's
    read-only Exists probe — so the never-move-or-delete-inside-a-Whisparr-root rule can hold across
    the WHOLE assembly, which is wider than the ingest slice the rule is written about. Renamer cannot
    carry these: relocating media is its purpose, and ~27 such calls live in its mover. A project is
    the boundary that separates the two, and only a project-local list can express that.
    RS0030's severity is also path-scopable through an .editorconfig section (measured: banned under
    Ingest/, allowed elsewhere) if a future ban ever needs a reach narrower than an assembly. It is
    unused here on purpose — the project boundary already isolates this one, and a second expression of
    one invariant is a second place for the two to disagree.
    NoMutationTests keeps stating the same rule as a readable assertion whose failure message explains
    it. The two are not redundant: this list matches the resolved symbol anywhere in the assembly
    (an alias defeats a source scan), while the test names the invariant in words.
    The blind options write is banned on the same assembly-wide reasoning: every options writer here derives
    its value from what is already stored, so none of them has a legitimate overwrite. Seeding a fresh store
    IS a legitimate overwrite, and every such call lives in a test project — which carries no list, so the
    project boundary separates the two here as well.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AdditionalFiles Include="BannedSymbols.WhisparrSync.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    This extension hashes nothing, so (unlike Renamer) there is NO System.IO.Hashing PackageReference and
    NO other bundled runtime dependency: the outbound HTTP stack (System.Net.Http, System.Text.Json,
    System.Net.Http.Json, Microsoft.Extensions.Http) is all host-provided shared framework carried by
    the FrameworkReference above and is never copy-local/bundled.

    Cove SDK resolution (local ProjectReference vs. NuGet PackageReference) is centralized in the root
    Directory.Build.targets — this project restates neither a direct Cove reference nor the sibling
    ../cove relative-path math (CLAUDE.md build-wiring rules).
  -->
</Project>
